at falls below Greece and  Spain a hundred thousand people each  year die from medical errors  ends up being about the sixth leading  cause of death in the United States and  we can't go on in this situation where  we're spending more and getting less  if you were re-elected President Obama  would fully implement the Affordable  Care Act which he passed during his  first term now the Affordable Care Act  really has twin aims okay the the first  is to make the US healthcare system more  efficient to reduce long-term spending  and the second is to expand access so in  order to make the US healthcare system  more efficient what what President Obama  is really proposing is to to do to  healthcare what Walmart did to the  manufacturing industry and that that is  to use the position of the government as  the single largest player in the health  sector to prompt providers to really  step up their game so first and foremost  he's changing the way he's paying  providers okay right now we have a  system that pays for quantity they're  hoping to shift that towards paying for  quality so for example if you're a  hospital with high readmission rates or  high infection rates what we're gonna  see is you're going to be paid less  under the Affordable Care Act now on the  insurance front in order to expand  coverage the president has done three  things the first thing that he's done is  mandated that everyone come in and buy  insurance and this is really with the  aim of getting these markets to function  more effectively you know what we don't  want to see is that folks who are  healthy waiting to get insurance until  they get sick and and really free-riding  off the system the second thing that  he's done is he subsidized insurance  coverage for the folks who previously  couldn't buy it and the third thing that  he's done is is expanded federal  programs and regulated the insurance  market okay so that insurance companies  can't do things like discriminating  against individuals 